+# Migrating from v1.1 to v1.2
+
+v1.2 is additive. The stable v1 `prelude`, `API_LEVEL = 1`, error variants, and
+existing v1.1 APIs remain unchanged.
+
+## Conditional distribution models
+
+Mean-only OLS remains appropriate when the conditional center and variance are
+the complete estimand. Use the new modules when that assumption is too narrow:
+
+- `quantile_regression` fits conditional quantiles by deterministic ADMM.
+- `expectile_regression` fits asymmetric least-squares expectiles.
+- `gamma_regression` models positive, right-skewed outcomes through a log link.
+- `location_scale` fits separate mean and log-variance predictor surfaces.
+- `mixed_effects` fits one Gaussian random intercept per observed group.
+
+These models expose convergence settings and reject rank-deficient or
+unsupported designs. Quantile/expectile coefficients are not interchangeable
+with conditional-mean coefficients.
+
+## Bayesian model comparison
+
+`bayesian_model_comparison` adds:
+
+- exact beta-binomial versus point-null Bayes factors,
+- known-variance normal-mean Bayes factors,
+- posterior model probabilities from log evidence and explicit model priors,
+- WAIC from a draw-major matrix of pointwise log likelihoods.
+
+WAIC requires at least two posterior draws and pointwise likelihood values. It
+is not computed from aggregate likelihoods.
+
+## Weighted networks
+
+Construct a `WeightedGraph` from a finite, non-negative square adjacency matrix.
+Undirected inputs must be symmetric within a caller-declared tolerance. Self
+loops are deliberately rejected in v1.2.
+
+- `network` provides connectivity, density, clustering, transitivity, and hops.
+- `network_centrality` provides degree/strength, closeness, PageRank, and
+  unweighted Brandes betweenness.
+- `network_inference` provides modularity, assortativity, matrix correlation,
+  and seeded QAP-style label permutations.
+
+Edge weights are strengths. Shortest hops and betweenness treat an edge as
+present when its weight is positive; they do not reinterpret strength as a
+metric distance.
+
+## Distributional scoring
+
+Use `distributional_scoring` to evaluate held-out distribution forecasts:
+
+- pinball loss for quantiles,
+- asymmetric squared loss for expectiles,
+- empirical quantile coverage,
+- Winkler interval scores and coverage,
+- Gaussian CRPS.
+
+Training data should not be reused as if it were held-out scoring data.
+
+## Verification
+
+Run the ordinary Rust lane with `./scripts/verify.sh` and independently
+regenerate the v1.2 Python references with
+`./scripts/verify_v1_2_references.sh`.
